# Trivy filesystem (Python dependency) baseline.
#
# Contract: explicit, dated and reviewable, exactly like
# `trivy-config.trivyignore`. Each entry carries `# expiry: YYYY-MM-DD` and
# `scripts/check_baselines_expiry.py` fails the build once past that date.
# Do NOT use trivy's own `expiredAt:` field — it is accepted and silently not
# honoured (measured at 0.71.0; see ADR-046).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
# ---------------------------
# The `Trivy filesystem scan` step was blocking (`exit-code: 1`) and had never
# examined a single Python dependency. Its own log said so on every run:
#
#     INFO  Number of language-specific files  num=0
#     WARN  [report] Supported files for scanner(s) not found.  scanners=[vuln]
#
# Trivy reads a requirements file only when versions are pinned exactly.
# This repository mandates `~=` — deliberately: numpy 2.x silently corrupts
# joblib models — and ships no lock file, so trivy had nothing to read.
# `scripts/resolve_python_dependencies.py` now resolves each requirements file
# with pip and hands trivy the concrete versions.
#
# Turning the gate on surfaced 24 fixable CRITICAL/HIGH findings that had been
# there all along. They are accepted HERE, with a date, rather than left
# invisible — and every NEW finding blocks immediately, which is the whole
# point of switching a dormant gate on instead of deleting it.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, stated rather than hidden: the plain ignore format
# suppresses a vulnerability id REPO-WIDE. If a second package were ever
# affected by the same CVE, this would silence it there too. Accepted for the
# same reason as the config baseline: trivy's YAML format supports `paths:`
# scoping but silently ignores `expiredAt:`, and one parser over one format is
# worth more than per-path precision.
#
# NONE of these is fixable within its declared pin range — every fix requires a
# MAJOR version bump, which is a product decision with its own test run, not a
# side effect of switching on a scanner. `.github/dependabot.yml` now watches
# pip, so those upgrades arrive as their own PRs with CI attached.


# --- mlflow — pinned `~= 2.18.0`, every fix is in 3.x ------------
#
# 20 findings ({'CRITICAL': 7, 'HIGH': 13}), installed 2.18.0.
# The largest cluster by far, and the one that most needs a decision rather
# than a bump. mlflow 3.x changes the tracking API surface the template's
# training and promotion paths depend on. Note also that mlflow is a
# DEVELOPMENT/tracking dependency here: it is not imported by the serving
# path (`app/`), so the exposure is the tracking server an operator runs,
# not the inference endpoint.
#
# Review question: has the mlflow 3.x migration ADR been written? If the
# answer is still no at expiry, that is the finding — not the CVE count.
#
# expiry: 2026-12-08  reason: mlflow 2.18.0 -> needs 3.4.0rc0 (major bump); HIGH
